## Edge cases

- Use exactly `###` for guideline headings. The parser expects that depth (`_HEADING_RE` in `python/architectural_guidelines.py`).
- Do not use `####`; markdownlint and `parse_guideline_entries` would reject or ignore it.
- Do not renumber `G-Py-10` to `G-Py-9`.
- Do not add code references that imply call-site verification was completed.
- Match bullet labels exactly: `- Why:` and `- Deviate when:` (case and spacing). Variants such as `- Deviate:` or `- deviate when:` are silently dropped by `parse_guideline_entries` while the heading still parses.

## Failure modes

- Adding `- Evidence:` creates inert content because the parser keeps only `Why` and `Deviate when`.
- Moving or restyling existing entries creates unnecessary churn.
- Adding mechanically-enforceable rules conflicts with `G-Enf-1` and the issue scope.
- A malformed `### G-*` heading (missing colon, wrong ID token, or `####` depth) can leave a guideline visible in the raw file but absent from `architectural-guidelines read` parsed output, so `/design` and `/implement` would silently miss an entry the acceptance criteria claim was added.
- A mistyped `- Why:` or `- Deviate when:` bullet on an otherwise valid heading produces the same silent-loss pattern: stdout can show `ARCHITECTURAL_GUIDELINES_STATUS=present`, all seven new IDs, and a passing ID-only grep while the parsed block for that entry lacks required body text.

## Testing strategy

- Run `make lint`.
- After the docs edit, run parser smoke verification:
  - `python3 python/cli.py architectural-guidelines read`
  - Require `ARCHITECTURAL_GUIDELINES_STATUS=present` on stdout.
  - Capture the parsed guideline body from the `architectural_guidelines` untrusted-content block on stdout (the normalized output of `parse_guideline_entries`, not the raw file).
  - Assert the captured body contains exactly 16 parsed entries (9 pre-existing + 7 new), for example by counting `### G-*:` headings in the capture.
  - For each new ID (`G-Py-7`, `G-Py-8`, `G-Py-10`, `G-Cfg-1`, `G-IO-1`, `G-CLI-1`, `G-Sec-1`), verify its contiguous parsed block (from its `### G-*:` heading through the line before the next `### G-*:` or EOF) includes both a `- Why:` line and a `- Deviate when:` line. ID-only `rg -F` checks against the full capture are insufficient.
  - Example manual slice check: for each new ID, extract that block from the capture and confirm both bullet labels appear within the slice only.
- No Python unit tests are required because this is a docs-only edit and no parser behavior changes.
- If lint fails, fix only markdown issues introduced by the new lines.

